          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly avows a present loving emotional state. Her following statement that the little prince did not know because of her fault frames the expression as a disclosure of a previously concealed feeling.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The avowal does not itself create, renew, or invoke an obligation. In the same farewell turn, the rose releases the little prince rather than positioning herself as bound to a continuing relational undertaking: she wishes him happiness, declines his protection, and accepts his departure. Her affection may persist, but no commitment is enacted by this occurrence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu as décidé de partir. Va-t'en.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression is presented as a calm, deliberate confession during a farewell, not as a reflex-like cry or uncontrolled affective discharge. The narration explicitly characterizes her manner as calm.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately captured as an avowal of love. Its contextual role as a belated revelation is part of that truth-presenting force rather than a distinct residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During the little prince's departure, the rose apologizes and calmly reveals that she loves him, explaining that her own fault prevented him from knowing. The confession occurs within a farewell in which she urges him to be happy and to leave rather than asking him to remain or securing a reciprocal pledge.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking is established by the focal expression. The rose avows concealed affection but simultaneously accepts the separation and relinquishes protective care under the globe. The little prince is surprised by her calm gentleness and continues expressing concern about wind and animals, but the supplied passage gives no explicit verbal acknowledgment or reciprocal commitment in response to the avowal.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognised as Chapter IX of Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene strongly supports the expression's avowal force and the rose's affection at this moment. It does not establish a reciprocal declaration, a negotiated future relationship, or whether the little prince fully understands and accepts the disclosure.\"}"
        }
      ],
